Step 0 has reached a decisive failure. In the paper’s genuine random-graph regime, Hamilton-cycle existence is an asymptotic probability theorem, not a distributional hardness theorem or a certificate-producing construction. The proposed inverse plant does give a witness, but it changes the distribution; in a dense version it is also routinely solved by standard search. I measured the natural two-cycle proposal at \(n=120\): a four-restart Hamilton search recovered two edge-disjoint cycles on 40/40 seeds (median 0.0112 s, 258 recursive states). There is also no Track B shortcut—the planted cycles are uniformly random secrets, not structure visible in the instance.
